## Does instrumental music improve studying?

### Decision, effect size, and report

::::: columns
::: {.column width="50%"}
::: callout-tip
## Decide

$t(38) = 1.73$, $p$ = .091. Since $p > .05$, we **fail to reject** $H_0$.
:::

::: callout-tip
## Effect size

$d = \dfrac{0.60}{1.09} = 0.55$ (a **medium** effect)
:::
:::

::: {.column width="50%"}
> An independent-samples t-test showed that students who studied with music (*M* = 7.65, *SD* = 1.08) did not differ significantly on the quiz from students who studied in silence (*M* = 7.05, *SD* = 1.11), [*t(38) = 1.73, p = .091*]{.hl}, 95% CI [-0.10, 1.30], *d* = 0.55.
:::
:::::

::: {.callout-tip appearance="minimal"}
A medium effect that is *not* significant is a hint that the study may have been too small to detect it. (If we had predicted a *direction* before collecting data, the one-tailed $p$ would be about 0.046. But the direction has to be chosen **before** you see the result.)
:::

## Does bedtime phone use affect sleep?

### Design

> **Design Summary**\
> 80 students from XYZ University took part in a study on sleep and cell phone use. Participants were randomly assigned to one of two conditions. One group was instructed to avoid phone use for 30 minutes before bed, while the other used their phones as normal. The next morning, all students rated their sleep quality on a 1-10 scale.

- **IV:** phone use before bed (yes vs. no)

- **DV:** sleep quality rating (1 = poor, 10 = excellent)

- **Design:** between-subjects, randomized

## Does bedtime phone use affect sleep?

### Hypotheses

::::: columns
::: {.column width="50%"}
::: callout-tip
## Null hypothesis $H_0$

Sleep quality is the same with and without bedtime phone use.

$\mu_\text{no phone} = \mu_\text{phone}$
:::
:::

::: {.column width="50%"}
::: callout-important
## Alternative hypothesis $H_A$

Sleep quality differs between the conditions.

$\mu_\text{no phone} \neq \mu_\text{phone}$
:::
:::
